{
  "info": {
    "name": "Lago Mock API v1.17.0",
    "description": "Test collection for the mock Lago service. Base URL defaults to http://localhost:8127. In docker-compose, the service is reachable at http://lago-api:8127.\n\nLago is **usage-based billing**, which makes it a different animal from the in-house payments service next door. Nothing here charges anyone.\n\n**You ingest events, not charges.** A usage event names a subscription, a billable-metric code and some properties. What it costs is computed later, by aggregating the period's events and running the plan's charges over the result.\n\n**The aggregation type decides what 'how much' means** -- `sum_agg` adds a property up, `max_agg` takes the peak, `unique_count_agg` counts distinct values, `count_agg` counts events. **The charge model decides what that number costs**, and the five models disagree wildly on the same units: `graduated` bills each tier at its own rate, `volume` finds the one tier the whole quantity falls in and bills all of it there, `package` sells blocks, `percentage` takes basis points of a monetary metric, `standard` is a flat per-unit rate.\n\n**`current_usage` exists before any invoice does** and is recomputed on every read -- it is the endpoint people actually integrate against.\n\n**Ingestion is idempotent on the caller's own `transaction_id`**: a repeat is refused rather than double-billed, and a batch is all-or-nothing.\n\nIdentity is dual: a `lago_id` the service owns and an `external_id` the caller owns. Responses are wrapped and errors carry a per-field `error_details` map, both because Lago's do. Amounts are integer cents; per-unit rates are **millicents** (1 cent = 1000) so a sub-cent rate stays exact. Requests labelled '(N expected)' assert an error path.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
